# UI DESIGN GUIDELINES

## 2. Layout & Structure (Trending)

Use:

• 12-column responsive grid
• Large breathing space
• 80–120px vertical section spacing
• Clean modular sections

Modern trend:
Centered content blocks with max-width ~1200px
Very clean alignment
No cluttered sidebars

White space is no longer empty space. It signals maturity.

---

## 3. Dark-First Design (High-End Trend)

Cybersecurity brands are mostly dark-mode dominant now.

Use:

Primary background: Deep navy
Elevated surfaces: Slightly lighter navy
Cards: Subtle elevation + soft border (1px low opacity)

Trend detail:
Very soft glass blur panels (subtle, not flashy)
Gradient overlays at section edges
Faint grid or dot-pattern background (extremely subtle)

Dark UI reduces eye strain and feels premium.

---

## 4. Typography (Very Important)

Trending typography for cybersecurity:

Large bold headlines
Clean geometric sans-serif

Recommended style:
• Headings: Bold, large, tight letter spacing
• Body: Medium weight, high readability
• Line height: 1.6–1.8

Hero headline example size:
48px–64px desktop

Modern trend:
Big statement headline.
Short subtext.
Primary CTA.
Secondary CTA outline.

No paragraph-heavy hero sections.

---

## 5. Button Design (Current Trend)

Primary Button:
• Solid electric blue
• 8–12px radius
• Subtle glow on hover
• Smooth 200ms transition

Hover trend:
Slight lift (translateY -2px)
Soft glow expansion

Avoid:
Sharp corners
Hard drop shadows
Heavy gradients

Soft elevation > harsh shadows.

---

## 6. Card Components

Services should be displayed in:

Minimal cards
Icon + Title + 2 lines max
Optional “Learn More” arrow

Trend:
Hover animation:
• Border glow
• Subtle scale 1.02
• Background lightening

Cards should feel interactive but controlled.

---

## 7. Motion & Microinteractions (Major 2026 Trend)

Subtle motion is expected now.

Use:

• Scroll-triggered fade-in
• Section reveal transitions
• Hover glow
• Button ripple or soft pulse

Avoid:
Heavy animations
Flying objects
Spinning cyber globes

Modern motion is quiet and smooth.

Speed:
200ms–400ms transitions

---

# UX DESIGN GUIDELINES

Now the important part. UX is where trust is built.

---

## 8. Clear Information Architecture

Your website should follow this simple structure:

Home
Services
Compliance & Frameworks
About
Contact

Avoid too many menu items.

Navigation should be sticky with subtle background blur.

---

## 9. Hero Section Formula (Trending Structure)

Left aligned content.

Headline:
“Illuminate Every Threat Vector.”

Subtext:
Clear, specific value proposition.

Two CTAs:
Primary: Book Consultation
Secondary: View Services

Optional:
Subtle animated background gradient.

No slider.
No rotating banners.
No clutter.

---

## 10. Social Proof Section

Even as a startup, structure it like this:

• Framework logos (ISO, SOC, NIST, etc.)
• “Trusted Approach” explanation
• Process breakdown in 3–4 steps

Trend:
Minimal logo strip with grayscale logos.
They turn color on hover.

---

## 11. Service Pages UX

Each service page should include:

Problem
Your Approach
What You Deliver
Outcome

Clear scannable structure.
No long essay paragraphs.

Add:
Small checklist sections with green indicators.

Security buyers scan quickly. They don’t read novels.

---

## 12. Visual Identity Trends in Cybersecurity

Currently trending:

• Deep gradients (blue → indigo → violet)
• Subtle glow edges
• Fine line icons (not filled bulky icons)
• Data-inspired abstract visuals
• Minimal 3D wireframe elements

Avoid:
Lock icons everywhere.
Binary rain.
Hacker hoodies.
Red warning alerts.

That aesthetic screams early 2010s.

---

## 13. Trust Signals UX

Modern trust signals include:

• Security-first language
• No exaggerated claims
• Transparent process explanation
• Clean privacy policy layout
• Visible contact details

Trust is built by clarity, not by shouting.

---

## 14. Performance & Technical UX (Very Important)

Cybersecurity sites must:

Load fast (<2 seconds ideal)
Be mobile perfect
Have smooth scrolling
Have accessible contrast ratios

Nothing kills credibility faster than a slow security website.

---

## 15. Mobile-First Reality

Most research begins on mobile now.

Mobile design must:

Have large touch targets
Clear CTA placement
No clutter
Readable font sizes

Buttons full-width on mobile.

---

